WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.640
How do you maintain cloud-specific code for an open-source platform that changes every single day?

00:06.120 --> 00:11.880
Welcome to Azure SPI, the engineering engine that keeps Microsoft's OSDU data platform running.

00:12.400 --> 00:17.720
Historically, shared repositories held updates back, so the community stripped out all cloud-specific code,

00:18.100 --> 00:21.620
leaving Microsoft as the sole owner and maintainer of the Azure implementation.

00:22.080 --> 00:23.800
But the community code keeps moving.

00:23.800 --> 00:30.300
To stay synchronized, Microsoft uses isolated repositories that automatically pull in those daily community updates.

00:30.700 --> 00:32.020
These are the service forks.

00:32.360 --> 00:38.060
They compile those open-source updates alongside Microsoft's proprietary Azure logic into a single container image.

00:38.380 --> 00:40.100
But a compiled image is just a candidate.

00:40.480 --> 00:42.760
You can't prove cloud code with simple unit tests.

00:43.080 --> 00:44.360
You need real infrastructure.

00:44.920 --> 00:48.260
Enter the stack, an on-demand, fully-provisioned Azure environment.

00:48.260 --> 00:56.520
The new container image temporarily borrows a slot in this live stack, executes its API-level acceptance tests directly against real Azure data services,

00:56.780 --> 00:58.720
and then restores the environment completely.

00:59.200 --> 01:02.220
By testing isolated cloud code against live infrastructure daily,

01:02.580 --> 01:06.200
Microsoft ensures its OSDU platform never falls behind the community standard.

